ise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:\nLet me analyze this situation carefully.\n\n**My Hand:** 5 of Spades, Queen of Clubs\n**Board:** Jack of Hearts, Queen of Spades, 3 of Hearts, 4 of Clubs, 10 of Diamonds\n\n**My Best Hand:** One Pair of Queens (Q-Q-J-10-5)\n\n**Key Observations:**\n1. This is the River (all 5 community cards are revealed) - final betting round\n2. I have one pair of Queens with Jack, 10, 5 kickers\n3. The board shows: J-Q-3-4-10 (no flush possible as suits are mixed, no obvious straight completed)\n4. Possible opponent hands to consider:\n   - Better pair (Kings, Aces) - would beat me\n   - Two pair or better - would beat me\n   - Pair of Jacks or lower - I win\n   - Straight: K-Q-J-10-9 or Q-J-10-9-8 or 9-8-7-6-5 or A-2-3-4-5 - opponent would need specific cards\n   - High card only - I win\n\n**Betting Pattern Analysis:**\n- Pre-flop: Both checked (suggesting neither has premium pocket pairs)\n- Flop (J-Q-3): I raised 100, opponent called (they didn't fold, suggesting some connection)\n- Turn (added 4): Both checked\n- River (added 10): Current decision\n\nThe opponent's call on the flop suggests they have something - possibly a Jack, a Queen, or a draw. Their check on the turn is interesting - they didn't raise with the 4 coming.\n\n**Risk Assessment:**\n- If opponent has AQ, KQ, or QJ (two pair), I lose badly\n- If opponent has AA, KK (overpair), I lose\n- If opponent has JJ (set), I lose badly\n- If opponent has worse Queen (Q9, Q8, Q7, Q6), we chop or I win on kicker\n- If opponent has just a Jack or missed draw, I win\n\nGiven the passive play (checks on turn after I showed aggression on flop), opponent likely doesn't have a monster. A value bet seems reasonable but risky.\n\nReason:\nWith one pair of Queens on the river, I have a marginal hand. The opponent's passive play (calling my flop bet but checking turn) suggests medium strength - possibly a weaker pair or draw. However, checking is the safest option here. A small value bet (100) could extract value from worse hands like pocket pairs below Queens or a Jack, but risks a check-raise from better hands. Given I'm already down overall and this is a marginal spot, checking to showdown is the prudent choice to minimize losses while still having showdown value.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
