d 2, you performed action: 0.\n* On Round 3, your opponent performed action: 0.\nYour valid actions are: [-2 (All-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:\nLet me analyze this situation carefully.\n\n**Current Hand Strength:**\n- My cards: King of Clubs, Jack of Hearts\n- Board: 8 of Spades, King of Hearts, 3 of Hearts, Queen of Spades, 4 of Spades\n- My hand: One Pair of Kings (K♣, K♥) with J, Q, 8 kickers\n\n**Board Analysis:**\n- There are 3 spades on board (8♠, Q♠, 4♠), making a flush possible\n- The board shows K-Q-8-4-3, so a straight is unlikely but possible (e.g., J-10-9 for opponent, though they'd need exactly J-10 or 10-9)\n- My pair of Kings is currently a decent hand\n\n**Opponent's Betting Pattern:**\n- Opponent has been passive throughout, just calling/checking on every street\n- Pre-flop: called my raise\n- Flop: called my raise\n- Turn: checked, I checked behind\n- River: checking to me again\n\nThis passive play suggests either:\n1. A weak hand/medium pair they're trying to showdown cheaply\n2. A trap with a very strong hand (unlikely given the checking pattern)\n3. A missed draw (possible flush or straight draw)\n\n**Pot Considerations:**\n- I've invested 400 (pre-flop) + 800 (flop) = 1,200 chips\n- Opponent matched these bets, so pot is approximately 2,400-2,500 chips\n- I have 18,700 chips remaining\n\n**Options:**\n1. **Check (0)**: Safe, goes to showdown. Pair of Kings might win against weaker pairs or missed draws.\n2. **Bet (~600-1000)**: Value bet to extract from worse Kings or pairs, also protects against opponent checking back something that beats us\n3. **Large Bet/All-in (-2)**: Risky and unnecessary with a medium-strength hand\n4. **Fold (-1)**: Makes no sense - checking is free and we might have the best hand\n\n**Key Insight:**\nGiven opponent's passive play throughout and the final check, they likely have a mediocre hand. My pair of Kings is reasonable. A moderate value bet (around 800-1000) could extract value from weaker pairs or a worse King, while not risking too much if they have a flush or two pair.\n\nHowever, checking is also reasonable to get to showdown safely, as the opponent's passivity could mean they're trapping, though this seems less likely.\n\nReason: \nMy opponent has shown extreme passivity throughout all betting rounds, just calling or checking. On the river, they checked again. My pair of Kings is a decent hand that likely beats bluffs, missed draws, and weaker pairs. A moderate value bet of 800 chips can extract value from hands like smaller pairs (99, 77) or worse Kings (K-9, K-7) while not overcommitting if they somehow have a flush or unlikely two pair. The pot is around 2,400 chips, so an 800 bet is a reasonable 1/3 pot sizing that gives good risk/reward.\n\nAnswer: 800"}
